1. ENCAPSULATION

Product.java: Protects data using private instance variables (id, name, price, quantity) with public getter and setter methods.

Food.java: Maintains a private calories field with a public getCalories() method.

PerishableFood.java: Uses a private expiryDate field with a public getExpiryDate() method.

InventoryManager.java: Encapsulates the stockList and totalRevenue variables, ensuring controlled access through specific methods.

2. INHERITANCE

Product.java: Serves as the abstract parent class.

Food.java: Implements Single Inheritance by extending the Product class.

PerishableFood.java: Implements Multi-level Inheritance by extending Food, which extends Product.

3. POLYMORPHISM (Method Overriding)

Food.java: Overrides the getDisplayInfo() abstract method from the Product class.

PerishableFood.java: Overrides getDisplayInfo() and utilizes super.getDisplayInfo() to include parent data.

4. ABSTRACTION

Product.java: Defined as an abstract class with an abstract method getDisplayInfo(), forcing implementation in all subclasses.

5. CLASSES & RESPONSIBILITIES

Product.java: Abstract base class for core item attributes.

Food.java: Concrete class representing non-perishable food items.

PerishableFood.java: Concrete class representing items with expiration dates.

InventoryManager.java: Service class managing inventory operations and revenue tracking.

Main.java: The execution entry point containing the system menu and user loop.

6. COMPOSITION

InventoryManager.java: Demonstrates Composition by containing an ArrayList of Product objects.

7. CONSTRUCTORS

Product.java: Implements a constructor with 4 parameters to initialize core attributes.

Food.java: Uses a 5-parameter constructor that calls the super constructor.

PerishableFood.java: Uses a 6-parameter constructor, chain-loading through super().

8. NON-STATIC / INSTANCE METHODS

InventoryManager.java: registerNewProduct(), displayReceipt(), sellProduct(), and restockProduct()